// The brotli package: Brotli (RFC 7932) decompression of a stream held in memory or fed in pieces. Written in
// Cone, with no C library and no call of the OS's itself; for streaming it imports streams and through it iobuf,
// whose buffer pool makes the OS calls.
//
//   mod unpack;
//   import stdio use *;
//   import brotli;
//
//   fn main() i32 {
//     let packed = [0x0Bu8, 0x00u8, 0x80u8, 0x58u8, 0x03u8];     // the stream for the one byte "X"
//     match brotli.decompress(&packed, 1usize) {
//       case imm ok Ok { printInt(i64.from(ok.len())); }              // 1
//       case imm e Err { printStr(e.reason.view()); return 1i32; }
//     }
//     0i32;
//   }
//
// WHAT IS HERE
//
//   ONE CALL         the stream in memory, the answer out
//     decompress(data, expected) Result[List[u8], BrotliError]
//   'expected' is a size hint: the output size if known (0 if not). It only reserves room, so a wrong hint
//   costs time and never correctness; the output is bounded by a limit of 1 GiB (setLimit, below).
//
//   A SETTINGS VALUE   for a run with other limits
//     Decompressor.make()
//       setLimit(bytes)        the most output allowed; more is refused as TooLarge
//       setMaxWindow(bits)     the largest window the stream may ask for (10 to 24, default 24: 16 MiB)
//       run(data, expected)    as decompress
//       totalIn(), totalOut(), isDone()   after a run, or as a stream goes
//
//   STREAMING        the same value, fed in pieces and pulled in pieces (a streams.Transform, so it goes
//                    in TransformStage[brotli.Decompressor]; deflate's Inflater and zstd's Decompressor are
//                    used the same way)
//       feed(chunk)            input of any size, split anywhere; it is copied
//       pull(&mut IoBuf)       output into the buffer's spare room, no more; answers Status (re-exported):
//                              NeedInput (all input used), MoreOutput (the room came first), Done, Failed
//       finish()               no more input will come (what is held is decoded; a cut stream is Truncated)
//       error(), failure()     why a pull answered Failed (BrotliError; the Transform's AnyError)
//       takeRest()             after Done: the input bytes that came after the stream's last meta-block
//
//   mut dc = brotli.Decompressor.make();
//   mut out = IoBuf(65536usize);
//   dc.feed(chunk);                     // any size, split anywhere
//   while true {
//     out.clear();
//     imm s = dc.pull(&mut out);
//     ... use out.view() ...
//     if s != Status.MoreOutput { break; }
//   }
//   // NeedInput: feed the next chunk, or dc.finish() and pull once more
//
//   FAILURE          BrotliError {kind, offset, reason}; ErrorKind is BadHeader, BadTable, BadCommand,
//                    BadDistance, BadTransform, Truncated, TooLarge, WindowTooLarge, NeedsLargeWindow
//
// THE DICTIONARY. Brotli's static dictionary (122,784 bytes) and its 121 transforms are part of the format, and
// are linked into every program that imports this package. They are generated from RFC 7932's own Appendices A
// and B by brotlitables.py, which checks them against the CRC-32 values the RFC states; a stream that names
// a word of every length with every transform (tests/data/own/dictwords.br) checks the compiled dictionary.
//
// HOW A PIECE CAN END ANYWHERE. Decoding goes a unit at a time: the stream header, a meta-block header as one
// unit, then a command's head, its run of literals, its distance, its copy (decoder.cone says how). Each is read
// through a 64-bit buffer that supplies zeros past the end of the input; if bits past the end were taken, the
// unit is put back and the pull answers NeedInput, or fails Truncated if the input is finished. A meta-block
// header may be tens of KB, and its length is not stated, so a header that runs out of input is looked at
// again only when the input held has at least doubled (or the input is finished): fed a byte at a time, a
// stream costs O(n log n) header work and not O(n^2). The price: a last meta-block header that ends exactly
// where the fed input ends is not decoded until finish(), and the stream completes at the pull after it.
// A run of literals, a copy and a dictionary word
// also stop where a pull's room ends and go on in the next pull. The one-call decode is the same machine, with
// the whole input held and finished from the start, and its whole output kept.
//
// MEMORY. A one-call run holds a copy of the input, the whole output, the prefix tables of the current
// meta-block (up to about 256 literal codes and 256 distance codes, a few hundred KB at the most) and, once a
// stream names a dictionary word, a 122,784-byte copy of the dictionary. A streamed run holds, in place of the
// output, a history of what a match may reach back to: the window the stream states (up to 16 MiB, WBITS 24),
// trimmed when it has grown a quarter of the window and 64 KiB past it, so at most about 20 MiB and one pull's
// output (a 10-bit window: about 66 KB). The input held is the part not yet used, which a header in doubt can
// make as large as the header, and the pieces fed and not yet pulled, in pool buffers (past the pool's bound,
// about 64 MiB, a feed fails the stream with TooLarge rather than panicking: feed in pieces, pull between).
//
// Not in RFC 7932 and refused: the large-window extension (NeedsLargeWindow) and shared dictionaries.

mod brotli;

import collections use *;
import iobuf use IoBuf;
// Status is re-exported as deflate and zstd do (the transform's answers)
import streams pub use Status;
